WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.760
Cobra Triangle, speed and skill are all that can save you.

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:27.360
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:28.900
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:30.260
And I'm Joe.

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:32.320
Guys, be honest with me.

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:37.620
When you saw that we were playing a game called Cobra Triangle, you must have thought at least

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:41.920
somewhat that it was going to be a playoff on the Bermuda Triangle.

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:43.860
Well, all right.

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:45.640
That's actually not what I was thinking.

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:51.440
I still don't know what Cobra Triangle is really referencing here.

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:55.660
I figured with Cobra in the name, I thought it was going to be like some kind of military

00:00:55.660 --> 00:01:01.500
thing because, you know, snakes show up in titles in those kinds of games sometime.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:03.660
But I have no idea what this means.

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:05.960
My original thought was similar.

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:09.420
Like maybe just because like Cobra Command type thing or something.

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:13.700
But once I saw there was water, I did think Bermuda Triangle.

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:15.600
Once I saw that there was water.

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:18.100
Yeah. But that had nothing to do with the Cobra Triangle.

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:20.220
Whenever I see water, I think about the Bermuda Triangle.

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:25.520
So now I think that the triangle part is just because like, you know, boats are slightly

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:27.440
triangular. Yeah.

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:31.380
And the Cobra part is because there's a Cobra in the wake.

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:34.120
When you look back behind a speedboat, it looks like a Cobra head.

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:35.960
Oh, that's fun.

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:41.320
Is it not that like are Cobra like a type of boats?

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:43.180
Like, is that a possibility?

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:45.160
I suppose. I don't know enough about boats.

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:47.160
I'm not rich enough to own one.

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:48.920
Joe's pretty confident about that.

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:50.520
I have no idea. Oh.

00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:52.740
Something about that.

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:58.640
Just, you know, maybe I just thought like even if maybe your boat is the Cobra in this

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:00.900
game, like for all we know, it's just nicknamed the Cobra.

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:08.040
And the triangle part is like just a, you know, it's a shape, just a fun shape reference.

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:09.780
What's your favorite shape?

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:12.960
It's just a reference to shapes, you know, a sick reference.

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:15.160
Why does it feel like we're done with the episode?

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:16.880
No, we're not done with the episode.

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:17.740
I feel like this is the post show.

00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:21.620
I think what I was really wanted this to be about was just about the Bermuda Triangle

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:27.260
because that is something that like as a kid you're told to be like actively afraid of.

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:30.800
Like we need to solve whatever's going down at the Bermuda Triangle.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:33.660
But then like when you become an adult, you just don't think about that anymore.

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:36.480
I mean, it's the same with whirlpools and that's in this game too.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:39.380
Like I was deathly afraid of whirlpools.

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:42.220
Whirlpools and quicksand.

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:43.400
Yeah, exactly.

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:45.580
They're everywhere but also nowhere.

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:51.360
But like, I mean, has anyone seen a whirlpool before?

00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:55.280
Has anyone seen a whirlpool and quicksand in the same room at the same time?

00:02:57.260 --> 00:02:58.800
All right, I'm going to move into the game.

00:02:58.940 --> 00:02:59.900
Please, please do.

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:04.020
Joe's right. We are bordering like a nonsensical conversation.

00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:09.220
I don't know. Cobra Triangle is a – I'm pulling this right from Wikipedia.

00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:10.260
I didn't come up with this.

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:13.640
It's a racing vehicular combat video game.

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:14.760
I think that sounds awesome.

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:18.300
I think it's also a couple of other genres too while we're at it,

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:19.960
but we'll get into that when we talk about the game.

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:26.560
Anyway, in Cobra Triangle, the player races a cannon-equipped speedboat against other watercrafts.

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:36.060
The 25 stages of graduated difficulty vary in objectives from winning races, saving swimmers, and diffusing bombs, and so much more.

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:45.560
Guys, I feel like I described many games but also the idea that all of these games happen while you drive a boat.

00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:50.880
Does that like – does that start the conversation at least enough for you guys?

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:53.260
Should we just talk about the boat to begin with?

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:57.260
I think so because it is – it's unexpected, you know?

00:03:57.420 --> 00:03:58.680
Like a speedboat.

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:01.540
We haven't really had much with like a speedboat type game.

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:08.460
You know, like this type of thing I would usually feel like would be a helicopter or a spaceship or a car or something.

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:15.480
But it's an interesting choice to make a speedboat, and I think it – the way it controls sort of feels like a speedboat.

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:19.980
So I think they – I think they had a nice match there.

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:25.900
Yeah, so basically the game is RC Pro-Am but on water.

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:33.560
And yeah, the fact that they used a boat – I don't – I definitely have not played too many games in general,

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:42.500
not even just in the realm of the NES, that your main form of movement is boat-based.

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:48.200
So it definitely gets points for how unique the concept is.

00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:51.040
But it's not just a boat.

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:55.460
Like the Wikipedia says, it's an armed boat.

00:04:55.720 --> 00:04:58.840
It has missiles and pew-pews.

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:05.720
But also between levels, like it just turns into a helicopter, which I think could have been explored in gameplay a bit.

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:11.920
Instead of just a way to get from pool to pool or whatever is happening here.

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:14.720
So I don't know.

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:15.860
That's how I see it.

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:21.340
I thought it was really funny that the helicopter element of it really does look like –

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:26.200
even in the world of the game, like that was designed as just utility to move the boat around.

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:30.360
Instead of hitching it to your trailer, just use the little helicopter to get it into the water.

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:36.060
Or just like cutting it to – cutting to black and like assuming that, okay, now you're in a different place.

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:39.380
It's like, well, we have to explain how the boat gets to this other body of water.

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:43.440
Everyone would be so confused if you don't see it go there.

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:44.980
Yeah. Right.

00:05:45.140 --> 00:05:56.680
Never mind that the helicopter would eliminate most of the challenge in this game if you could just use it during sections like jumping over waterfalls and, you know, avoiding, you know, armed artillery.

00:05:57.800 --> 00:06:14.820
Right. But, you know, Joe, I want you to talk a little bit about how this particular boat, this – you know, we're going to call it the Cobra – controls because, you know, as you mentioned, this is our first time we're dealing with a boat being the main character.

00:06:15.220 --> 00:06:20.320
And obviously like, you know, the water itself plays a large role in how you control this boat.

00:06:20.620 --> 00:06:24.900
So if you wouldn't mind, just talk a little bit about how it feels to be the Cobra.

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:42.200
Yeah. Well, right off the bat, I think that this is – you know, it's just – this is one of those games that makes me think like why can't more games get the feel of the way you control whatever, your vehicle, your person or whatever, as good as this game did.

00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:44.600
Like this feels smooth.

00:06:44.980 --> 00:06:45.960
It's one-to-one.

00:06:46.120 --> 00:06:46.960
It never lags.

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:50.180
It doesn't feel like you're trudging ever, if that makes any sense.

00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:58.360
So I think like right off the bat, I want to give my kudos to this game because I think this feels – it's one of the few games that we've had that feels beyond the NES.

00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:09.440
You know, it feels like, you know, you get air off of ramps, you skip off of waves, you really have a lot of fluid motion in this, and I think it works really nicely.

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:18.300
The other thing that you'll probably notice right away when controlling this is it has that control scheme where your perspective as the player doesn't change.

00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:25.720
But – so like whenever you're – when you're turning right, you have to think about the fact that you're turning to the driver's right.

00:07:25.900 --> 00:07:26.800
Yeah, tank controls.

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:31.480
If you're moving back towards the camera, you have to kind of reverse everything in your head.

00:07:31.640 --> 00:07:34.080
So like that takes some getting used to, at least for me.

00:07:34.220 --> 00:07:40.980
It always does in games like that, but it's not, in my opinion, it's not necessarily like a huge flaw.

00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:44.080
I think it's just something that like I'm not used to and it takes some time to get used to.

00:07:44.500 --> 00:07:53.260
Yeah, I think the – I think that the tank controls and the isometric view really work well together.

00:07:53.260 --> 00:08:00.580
I mean this – again, I mentioned RC Pro-Am because this is, I think, built on like the same code, I imagine.

00:08:02.140 --> 00:08:07.020
I thought that that felt really fluid as well when we talked about that game.

00:08:07.020 --> 00:08:10.820
And now all it's doing is sort of like messing with momentum a bit.

00:08:12.920 --> 00:08:20.900
And I just think that like this – that whole perspective and control scheme, like just what Joe was saying,

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:30.480
it kind of makes it feel a bit more timeless because it's not like – we're not worried about like,

00:08:30.620 --> 00:08:34.740
oh, this jump feels kind of stiff or anything.

00:08:34.740 --> 00:08:36.100
It's just like one-to-one.

00:08:37.760 --> 00:08:44.640
Right, and I think, you know, some people who like pick up this game and start playing it might be inclined to say that like,

00:08:44.740 --> 00:08:52.320
no, it's very difficult to control this boat and also like, you know, because it is a tank, like be a shmup at the same time.

00:08:52.460 --> 00:08:55.840
You know, like it's very hard to do both of those things in this space.

00:08:55.840 --> 00:09:06.040
But I think something about the way that the momentum of moving the boat and the aliveness of the water, you know,

00:09:06.140 --> 00:09:11.120
like even though it's not perfectly demonstrated to you with the pixel art,

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:16.880
I do feel like they're giving a good amount of momentum to the waves themselves

00:09:16.880 --> 00:09:21.900
and to how it would be in a speed boat when you're charging all that,

00:09:21.900 --> 00:09:25.380
that you just kind of get that timing down with practice.

00:09:25.720 --> 00:09:29.420
You know, it's not that the – it's not that it's hard to control the Cobra.

00:09:29.640 --> 00:09:33.660
Yes, at times like you may have given yourselves too many speed upgrades

00:09:33.660 --> 00:09:37.560
and therefore, you know, you're kind of punished for being – for going too fast.

00:09:37.720 --> 00:09:45.820
But I feel like overall just the control of the Cobra and the way that they built the physics, let's call it,

00:09:45.940 --> 00:09:51.020
of how it moves in all of the water stages is one, it's consistent, which is nice.

00:09:51.020 --> 00:09:57.360
But also too, it's something where you're rewarded for playing it more often

00:09:57.920 --> 00:10:00.380
and kind of like getting that timing down.

00:10:00.620 --> 00:10:05.100
I'm thinking specifically about when you first get to like the obstacle course stages

00:10:05.100 --> 00:10:08.160
where there's like log flumes and stuff like that coming at you.

00:10:08.340 --> 00:10:12.720
That can be a very hard thing if you're just charging your way through those stages, right?

00:10:12.980 --> 00:10:19.600
Oh, yeah. I mean like there's a difference between a game being difficult

00:10:19.600 --> 00:10:23.520
and a game just having poor controls making it difficult.

00:10:23.760 --> 00:10:29.180
And this is sort of that – this is an example of that where it's almost –

00:10:29.180 --> 00:10:32.040
for me it was almost second nature controlling this vehicle,

00:10:32.040 --> 00:10:35.500
but that does not mean that this game was easy in any way

00:10:35.500 --> 00:10:37.480
because of the way that the levels are designed.

00:10:37.480 --> 00:10:40.440
And sometimes I feel like they're designed kind of unfairly.

00:10:40.580 --> 00:10:42.120
But you're right, Mike.

00:10:42.220 --> 00:10:45.600
Like when you – if you do keep playing it, you will pick up on it.

00:10:45.720 --> 00:10:50.560
And it is those optical course levels, if we want to call it that,

00:10:52.100 --> 00:10:55.180
that were the biggest frustration inducer for me.

00:10:55.780 --> 00:10:59.420
Yeah. I think that – I think as far as the – as far as controlling the boat,

00:10:59.460 --> 00:11:04.060
I think it's difficult in the manner that they intended it to be difficult.

00:11:04.060 --> 00:11:06.160
You know, it's not difficult because of poor controls.

00:11:06.320 --> 00:11:09.380
It's difficult because they wanted it to be difficult, you know.

00:11:11.480 --> 00:11:14.900
Yeah, like you almost need a certain – it would just be too –

00:11:15.420 --> 00:11:18.080
it wouldn't be interesting enough if the game just was like,

00:11:18.160 --> 00:11:21.580
yeah, that's the boat in it and it just moves at, you know,

00:11:21.680 --> 00:11:24.600
a same fluid speed that makes it easy to navigate around.

00:11:24.720 --> 00:11:29.040
Like I understand that, that it does kind of need some kind of maneuverability challenge

00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:30.600
to make it interesting to be the boat.

00:11:30.600 --> 00:11:35.780
You know, you're not just on land in a car going in a straight line.

00:11:35.780 --> 00:11:37.380
You're in a body of water.

00:11:37.980 --> 00:11:42.060
And that's why the turning is really interesting with the boat here

00:11:42.060 --> 00:11:47.560
because I feel like even just the pixel art for the turns sells it enough

00:11:47.560 --> 00:11:51.400
that I feel like it's all happening not only very fluid,

00:11:51.400 --> 00:11:54.580
but in a way that like it's easy for my brain to digest.

00:11:54.580 --> 00:12:01.460
Like, you know, how I can avoid obstacles and how I can line myself up with the ramps

00:12:01.460 --> 00:12:02.900
and the power-ups and such.

00:12:03.100 --> 00:12:10.180
Yeah, I mean, can you imagine playing this game if like the controls were actually based on

00:12:10.180 --> 00:12:14.860
your perspective instead of the boat's perspective?

00:12:14.860 --> 00:12:20.540
I think it would just feel, it either wouldn't feel boat-like

00:12:20.540 --> 00:12:25.180
or it would just be completely disorienting, I think.

00:12:25.520 --> 00:12:28.600
Yeah, I think because there's so much like turning and flipping around

00:12:28.600 --> 00:12:31.080
and, you know, suddenly you're going one way and then you're turning the other way.

00:12:31.180 --> 00:12:34.940
I can't even picture how the game would do that, you know?

00:12:35.100 --> 00:12:37.860
Like, what if you're kind of halfway between one, you know,

00:12:37.920 --> 00:12:39.960
there's only four directions on the D-pad.

00:12:40.260 --> 00:12:43.940
Yeah. So like it feels weird to even imagine it that way.

00:12:46.200 --> 00:12:52.560
And then the second part to, you know, all of the reasons to make the boat different, right,

00:12:52.620 --> 00:12:55.220
to be its own character and stuff like that, are these power-ups,

00:12:55.700 --> 00:12:58.340
which are handled pretty interesting in this game

00:12:58.340 --> 00:13:03.500
where there are these pods that are in certain levels that you collect them

00:13:03.500 --> 00:13:07.540
and each one that you collect either has like a number one or a number two over it

00:13:07.540 --> 00:13:12.320
and at the bottom of your screen there are different lists of power-ups

00:13:12.320 --> 00:13:16.380
and it's all handled very similar to how Gradius handled its power-ups

00:13:16.380 --> 00:13:22.060
where, you know, if you collect a pod, you can select that power-up

00:13:22.060 --> 00:13:25.900
or you can keep collecting pods to get a potentially better power-up

00:13:25.900 --> 00:13:29.920
but you can only, once you spend your power-up, you spend your power-up points

00:13:29.920 --> 00:13:33.260
and you go back to the beginning and are forced to like recollect.

00:13:33.800 --> 00:13:37.420
I know it sounds like a lot, but really if you just think about how Gradius and Lifeforce handled it,

00:13:37.540 --> 00:13:42.560
it's kind of funny to see that show up in what is described as a boat racing game,

00:13:42.580 --> 00:13:48.180
but I think it works here, you know, in like the same reason why it worked in Gradius.

00:13:48.280 --> 00:13:52.360
You're given the challenge in your choice of like what you think you need

00:13:53.280 --> 00:13:56.420
for the particular stage and also for upcoming stages, right?

00:13:56.540 --> 00:14:00.640
Like I think the first time I played through this game, I just wanted to be faster

00:14:00.640 --> 00:14:05.180
and then I realized when I got to like the boss fights, I was like, I need to be stronger.

00:14:05.180 --> 00:14:07.220
Like I'll never take these things down.

00:14:08.020 --> 00:14:10.300
So, you know, you have to decide that stuff.

00:14:10.740 --> 00:14:14.680
What did you guys think about the, you know, not just the power-ups in general,

00:14:14.800 --> 00:14:19.420
but specifically that mirroring of the Gradius element to it?

00:14:19.420 --> 00:14:31.660
I agree that like the first playthrough that I did with this game, like I was kind of not paying attention to the action bits of it.

00:14:32.660 --> 00:14:41.500
That I was just sort of treating it as a more of a, I'm running an obstacle course and I just need to get to the end

00:14:41.500 --> 00:14:47.600
and less like I need to deal damage like with like any of the bosses.

00:14:48.580 --> 00:15:00.420
And I still think that like when I, the levels that focus more on maneuvering and speed, like I like those better,

00:15:00.620 --> 00:15:08.820
but I was able to appreciate the more action focused bits once I did start paying attention to my power-ups

00:15:08.820 --> 00:15:16.220
and actually focusing on getting like a wider spray or like missiles or things like that.

00:15:16.520 --> 00:15:20.880
It was a, it was a bit more fun when you, it's always more fun when you're, when you're powerful.

00:15:21.180 --> 00:15:28.260
So it was an aspect of the game that I, I guess I didn't realize I was missing until I played it two or three times.

00:15:28.600 --> 00:15:35.500
Yeah. I actually was kind of the same way I got to the, so I got to like the second boss without really doing much upgrading of like my missiles and everything.

00:15:35.900 --> 00:15:37.460
And could not, I was like locked.

00:15:37.640 --> 00:15:42.000
I was like, okay, there's no way I'll ever get past this unless I play for a really long time.

00:15:42.260 --> 00:15:46.640
And then when I started over and played that way, it, yeah, it makes a huge difference.

00:15:46.640 --> 00:15:50.520
And it really feels like you're customizing your, you know, you're customizing your character.

00:15:50.640 --> 00:15:58.640
It feels like RPG-like, but it's not, yeah, it's not, I like, I like the fact that it's not as simple as like,

00:15:58.760 --> 00:16:00.080
okay, where do I want to spend my point?

00:16:00.080 --> 00:16:06.500
That you have to do like a skill-based thing to get yourself, like just to select what you want to upgrade.

00:16:06.700 --> 00:16:08.460
Then you upgrade it, then you got to do that again.

00:16:08.460 --> 00:16:18.640
It like kind of adds to the, to like how important those things feel and how like your appreciation of them after,

00:16:18.640 --> 00:16:23.080
after spending like your, spending your time and skill and effort to get them.

00:16:23.080 --> 00:16:29.420
Yeah, between what's going on for the particular stage that you're in, because remember the objective keeps changing.

00:16:29.900 --> 00:16:35.280
So between that, the collecting of the power-ups and deciding your power-ups,

00:16:35.380 --> 00:16:42.820
and then also the short burst nature of each individual level, there really is a frantic nature to the game, right?

00:16:42.940 --> 00:16:47.160
There's a lot going on when you're actually playing it.

00:16:47.160 --> 00:16:53.120
And then they give you kind of these, these moments where it's like, okay, stage is over, here's four seconds to like get it together.

00:16:53.120 --> 00:16:57.840
And then we're going into the next new objective, learn and adapt quickly.

00:16:58.380 --> 00:17:03.300
I think that the power-ups, you know, make the whole game a lot more interesting.

00:17:03.800 --> 00:17:13.600
If you, if you played, if you even tried to play without them, I think you just get something that is either too hard or also just like takes away the, the fun of it, right?

00:17:13.600 --> 00:17:24.180
Like I think having a boat that shoots like missiles and, and shoots in like eight different directions and has a force field, like there's something cool and interesting about that.

00:17:24.280 --> 00:17:27.080
That's what gives some personality to this rare game.

00:17:27.080 --> 00:17:36.840
But I think the, the way that you get the power-ups and I'm not suggesting that it needed to be like a, based on score or something like that,

00:17:36.940 --> 00:17:50.820
but the pods are only available in certain levels and they're given out kind of sparingly other than a particular level that is just called collect the pods, you know, which, which reoccurs.

00:17:51.040 --> 00:17:51.960
It's not like it's just one.

00:17:51.960 --> 00:17:53.180
That's like a bonus level.

00:17:53.340 --> 00:17:57.080
Yeah. It seems like a bonus level, but it's also like, that's your only moment.

00:17:57.200 --> 00:18:12.840
And I feel like at that rate, like it should, it should almost because of, you know how, like when you create a character in a game and I think we had this with Nobunaga's ambition where you are creating your character based on certain attributes.

00:18:12.860 --> 00:18:19.180
And then you get to a particular set of skills and it's all of a sudden it's just like RNG decides what that is.

00:18:19.180 --> 00:18:21.220
And it's like, well, I don't like those results.

00:18:21.220 --> 00:18:24.260
So now I got to reset my whole character and make another one.

00:18:24.260 --> 00:18:35.840
I feel like the way that the power-ups are given out in this game, it could be an early thing where it's like, well, I didn't collect enough of those pods and I don't like my loadout.

00:18:35.840 --> 00:18:38.960
So I'm better off resetting than continuing.

00:18:39.420 --> 00:18:51.520
And I'm not saying like that they needed to make them easier to collect or anything, but they should have been something that could be acquired, you know, throughout each of the stages.

00:18:51.840 --> 00:18:58.060
Now, I will mention the way that they handle power-ups in this is a lot better than how Gradius does.

00:18:58.060 --> 00:19:02.920
If you die, you do keep all your power-ups, which I think is a huge plus.

00:19:04.120 --> 00:19:05.820
Maybe that was like their trade-off.

00:19:06.020 --> 00:19:10.660
Like you won't be able to collect a lot of these, but we're also not going to punish you for dying once.

00:19:10.700 --> 00:19:13.960
Like we're not going to strip you of every single power-up that you have.

00:19:14.580 --> 00:19:26.760
So, you know, I guess I'm saying like, you know, maybe I'm talking myself out of it, but also like it was weird to see that like even in the first stage, you can go that entire thing without collecting a power-up.

00:19:26.880 --> 00:19:32.420
And then all of a sudden you're given a bunch and then there's two more stages where no power-ups can possibly be collected.

00:19:32.720 --> 00:19:39.640
It just doesn't feel like they're putting as much emphasis on the power-ups as say you need in this particular game.

00:19:39.640 --> 00:19:50.620
Yeah, I mean, I guess like one way that they could have done it was it's like score-based and that has like the added benefit of actually giving a reason to worry about score.

00:19:52.540 --> 00:20:08.620
But unless they wanted to just do like more pickups or just like that, there are not always these pods that are trailing, that are trailing like the enemy boats that they just sort of like littered across the map.

00:20:08.620 --> 00:20:19.600
I don't know how they could have done it to sort of balance it out, but I do agree that it does feel kind of streaky where you can find them.

00:20:20.360 --> 00:20:26.100
I don't hate that they're so scarce or like that they're only in those first, because they're in the first two levels.

00:20:26.160 --> 00:20:33.080
The first level you can get one or two of them and then the second level, if I'm remembering correctly, the collect the pods I think is the second level.

00:20:33.080 --> 00:20:36.460
Whenever the collect the pods is, you can get a bunch of them.

00:20:36.700 --> 00:20:43.600
And I don't hate it as long as the player is aware that like that those are the two levels.

00:20:43.680 --> 00:20:50.920
You know, like I feel like the first time you play it, it's kind of frustrating because you're like, OK, well, I didn't know that I was going to have to go six more levels before I was going to be able to upgrade anything again.

00:20:51.380 --> 00:20:55.480
That's annoying. But I feel like as long as like on your second playthrough.

00:20:56.920 --> 00:21:06.440
Like with anything, with anything that that involves a strategy with any game, I think that like it's important in for as far as like fairness to the player that they know the rules.

00:21:06.660 --> 00:21:17.600
You know what I mean? They know like not that it's a rule, but like it feels like if you know that going in, I feel like it's kind of a cool mechanic where you're like, OK, the first the first level, I'll collect one or two.

00:21:18.100 --> 00:21:19.340
Get what I want in the second level.

00:21:19.440 --> 00:21:29.300
OK, this is my time to like build myself as much as I can and like kind of like save up for the winter because in the next five levels, I'm going to need these power ups.

00:21:29.400 --> 00:21:33.580
So like, all right, this one I'm going to do this level is collect as many of these things as I can get my power ups.

00:21:33.620 --> 00:21:36.720
Now I got to use this to get me through to the next one, which will be a little while.

00:21:36.920 --> 00:21:38.580
And I don't think that's such a bad thing.

00:21:38.740 --> 00:21:46.700
I just think that that like being blindsided by it doesn't give you the opportunity to like use it as a gameplay mechanic.

00:21:46.700 --> 00:22:00.480
But you don't think that to the to the other point I was making earlier that like so now that you know that it's available in the first two stages, if you don't perform well in those first two stages, you're just going to reset.

00:22:00.680 --> 00:22:06.620
Right. To make sure that you collect all the pods in the second stage, that you have a more well equipped boat going.

00:22:06.720 --> 00:22:14.820
I mean, I guess I see that and I don't see the like, you know, it's hard to see the like the incentive to not do that because it's so early in the game.

00:22:15.660 --> 00:22:21.700
But, you know, it is also just like depends on how, you know, how you want to play the game.

00:22:21.860 --> 00:22:30.680
I mean, if you I don't know, it feels like that doing that kind of you're robbing yourself a little bit of playing the game as the way it was intended to be played.

00:22:31.520 --> 00:22:33.960
But I get it like it's so early in the game.

00:22:33.960 --> 00:22:44.600
I mean, it's just like when, you know, if I'm trying to if I'm trying to beat a boss and I and I suddenly in like some other game and I suddenly like get my ass handed to me in the first 10 seconds.

00:22:44.660 --> 00:22:46.620
I'm like, OK, I'm just going to start. So I'm going to hit reset and start over.

00:22:46.780 --> 00:22:47.640
Like I get that.

00:22:47.940 --> 00:22:49.020
But yeah, I don't know.

00:22:49.100 --> 00:22:53.840
I think it just comes down to like if you want to like if the player wants to like let themselves play it that way.

00:22:54.480 --> 00:22:55.760
Yeah. Yeah. I hear you.

00:22:55.920 --> 00:23:02.860
And again, like I don't think it has to be something where or it definitely shouldn't be something where, OK, you completed this stage.

00:23:03.020 --> 00:23:05.280
Here's your power up reward like that.

00:23:05.420 --> 00:23:07.320
That sticks. That's not fun.

00:23:07.440 --> 00:23:15.080
And that's not even like that's not necessarily like it's not that kind of game where you should be rewarded with a with becoming stronger because you completed a level.

00:23:15.080 --> 00:23:19.820
It should be something tied to skill because ultimately that's what this whole game is, right?

00:23:19.880 --> 00:23:20.760
It's a test of skill.

00:23:20.800 --> 00:23:27.260
I mean, in some instances, like you're on you're not completing the level unless you have some modicum of skill.

00:23:27.260 --> 00:23:38.660
I mean, especially the ones with like waterfalls and whirlpools like that's skillful to be able to finish, you know, I guess without using a continue.

00:23:39.320 --> 00:23:47.680
Oh, definitely. Oh, so you're but you're saying like if you complete that level that it might be it might be worth giving out like a power up for that.

00:23:48.420 --> 00:23:51.880
I mean, it doesn't seem counterintuitive.

00:23:52.160 --> 00:23:53.420
I think that it's no, I'm not.

00:23:53.480 --> 00:23:56.500
I'm not. I really honestly I could go one way or the other with this.

00:23:56.500 --> 00:24:10.840
I guess what I what I'm thinking is is like because it's a it's a game of like, you know, you're being tested by the objectives continuing to change and by how much you know how little damage you can take and stuff like that.

00:24:10.840 --> 00:24:18.860
That that the power ups being tied to a additional bonus that you can collect is arguably like more.

00:24:20.280 --> 00:24:26.220
It's more cohesive to what the game is setting up for you than handing you these power ups, right?

00:24:26.340 --> 00:24:37.000
Like if they do it that way, it's almost like the the test is is being is being levied by the game, not by the players skill.

00:24:37.000 --> 00:24:43.660
So, yeah, I suppose I know what you're saying, too, because I feel like I mean, I'm not saying one way is better than the other.

00:24:43.780 --> 00:24:52.140
I just feel like the way this game is just what it's trying to do is make it, you know, make it feel like you're you know, yeah, you're in control of how powerful you end up being.

00:24:52.300 --> 00:25:03.080
And like if you were to go the other route or like beating a level gives you a power up, then like always on the third level, you'd always have three power ups, you know, like you'd always be at this level of power.

00:25:03.700 --> 00:25:10.220
Or at, you know, at this stage and as you go on, you it would kind of be like, well, I guess that also depends on that.

00:25:10.440 --> 00:25:22.520
I'd say like if it were some sort of like hybrid of the two, like you could have only done enough to get the one guaranteed for completing X level.

00:25:22.520 --> 00:25:26.880
Like you can still get more and be more powerful because you are skillful.

00:25:27.020 --> 00:25:34.720
But it would also alleviate the effect of, well, I I don't have any power ups.

00:25:34.720 --> 00:25:41.700
And now I'm in the middle of these these couple levels where they just don't hand them out at all.

00:25:42.880 --> 00:25:56.880
It would it would sort of make it so that you're not immediately restarting just because of the power up situation that you're in at this specific point where you're already in a suboptimal state.

00:25:56.880 --> 00:25:59.440
I don't know. It's not entirely important.

00:25:59.720 --> 00:26:09.200
But if if if we want to debate the the philosophy of of power up acquirement, I guess it's it's it's it's in there.

00:26:09.980 --> 00:26:11.220
Yeah, no, I hear it.

00:26:11.240 --> 00:26:14.000
And I think that what you're saying works.

00:26:14.320 --> 00:26:17.120
I just think that that changes the game.

00:26:18.080 --> 00:26:23.620
So much like not not so much, but it changes the game as to like what I like.

00:26:23.620 --> 00:26:28.240
Here's like an analogy that, you know, I always like to have a Kingdom Hearts analogy every few episodes.

00:26:29.360 --> 00:26:37.980
So in Kingdom Hearts one, you beat when you beat a boss, you get the you get the appropriate amount of experience points.

00:26:38.000 --> 00:26:40.480
And from those experience points, you get whatever levels you get.

00:26:40.560 --> 00:26:42.080
Sometimes you'll get you'll because it was a boss.

00:26:42.200 --> 00:26:46.920
It's a ton of experience. You get you get three or four levels and you, you know, or sometimes if you're further ahead,

00:26:46.920 --> 00:26:50.200
you get fewer levels or whatever, just depending on on where you are.

00:26:50.640 --> 00:26:54.340
And Kingdom Hearts two, they change that where when you beat a boss, you don't get experience.

00:26:54.420 --> 00:26:59.640
You get automatic like level up style, like level up worthy bonuses.

00:27:00.460 --> 00:27:02.560
So it's like always in that game.

00:27:02.740 --> 00:27:05.700
When you beat this boss, you'll get this much health.

00:27:06.060 --> 00:27:08.320
And it's like all in like, which is fine.

00:27:08.480 --> 00:27:12.900
And I feel like it probably makes it easier for them to design the game in a fun way.

00:27:13.300 --> 00:27:16.820
But for me as the player, it does make me feel like I've lost a little bit of the freedom.

00:27:17.200 --> 00:27:20.460
I feel like no matter what, when I'm at this stage in the game, this is how much health I'll have.

00:27:20.800 --> 00:27:28.300
Whereas in Kingdom Hearts one, it was always cool for me to like, see like what the see the results of my grinding were like,

00:27:28.400 --> 00:27:30.400
oh my God, look how much health I have so early in the game.

00:27:31.080 --> 00:27:33.400
You know, so it's like, yeah, I can see it both ways.

00:27:33.400 --> 00:27:35.400
But yeah, that's where I'm coming from.

00:27:37.100 --> 00:27:43.000
I also just think, you know, and we should move on to the other objectives here because this is going to lead well into it,

00:27:43.140 --> 00:27:48.080
is that ultimately the game is just giving you like a test of your skill, right?

